Confirmed: the two refetch/reconcile writes at 2073-2074 and 2077-2078 (sections = fresh / sections = before) are each gated by if (g !== routeLoad.generation) return;. If either guard fires, the function returns immediately from inside the inner try/catch, so it never reaches line 2083 at all for that stale-generation case. But the terminal writes removeError = {...} (2083) and failedRemoveId = id (2084) themselves carry NO generation check of their own — they are reached (and execute unconditionally) whenever the inner refetch/reconcile guards did NOT trigger an early return, i.e. in the ordinary same-collective failure case, which is correct — but there is no defensive re-check at 2083 itself, unlike every other terminal write in this file's other guarded handlers. Additionally, the finally block's own side effect at line 2094 — if (ownsFocus && failedRemoveId !== null) await placeFocusAfterFailedRemove(failedRemoveId); — is also NOT guarded by g (only the removePending flag-clear at 2093 is, via if (g === routeLoad.generation)), so a stale failed-remove can still move DOM focus after a collective switch even though the removePending-flag clear beside it is correctly guarded. Available g: const g = routeLoad.generation; at line 2007, already in scope for the whole function including catch and finally. The refetch reconcile branches (2073, 2077) and the removePending clear (2093) already reuse it; removeError/failedRemoveId (2083-2084) and the finally's focus-placement call (2094) do not.
